   "id": "9701-13.4-x012",
   "key": "D",
   "solver": "C",
   "tiebreak": "C",
   "status": "closed",
   "resolution": "fix: Retain answer D. In build/work/mcq/overrides.json under qid 9701_s20_11_q28, set explanation to: \"There are seven chiral centres (D): the methyl-bearing quaternary carbon at the upper-right vertex of the leftmost six-membered ring, the four substituted CH carbons of the central six-membered ring, and the two CH carbons shared by the five-membered ring and the four-membered lactone ring. At the quaternary centre, the two paths around the leftmost ring are different: one reaches the carbon bearing two methyl groups sooner than the other. A chiral carbon need not carry a hydrogen, so omitting this centre gives the incorrect count of six (C). The carbon bearing two methyl groups and the branched carbon in the ester side chain are not chiral because each has two identical methyl groups.\""
  },
  {
   "id": "9701-13.4-x016",
   "key": "C",
   "solver": "D",
   "tiebreak": "D",
   "status": "closed",
   "resolution": "fix: Retain answer C. In build/work/mcq/overrides.json under qid 9701_s20_12_q30, set explanation to: \"The three structural products obtained by adding one HBr across each original double bond are 1,4-dibromobutane, 1,3-dibromobutane and 2,3-dibromobutane; reversing the chain does not give an additional structural isomer. 1,4-Dibromobutane, BrCH2CH2CH2CH2Br, has no chiral centres. 1,3-Dibromobutane, BrCH2CH2CHBrCH3, has one, and 2,3-dibromobutane, CH3CHBrCHBrCH3, has two. Therefore two products have at least one chiral centre (C). The count is of qualifying structural products, not the total of three chiral centres across those products; D is incorrect.\""
  },
